        {
          "label": "Anchor · opening",
          "voice": "Microsoft Zira Desktop",
          "visual": "studio",
          "title": "When the mission changed",
          "headline": "ENDURANCE SPECIAL REPORT · 1914–1916",
          "points": ["How did the plan change?", "How did people respond?"],
          "credit": "A present-day classroom report looking back at historical evidence",
          "narration": [
            "Welcome to Expedition News Network. Today we are looking back at the Endurance expedition, from 1914 to 1916.",
            "Our question is: how did a plan to cross Antarctica become a mission to bring people home?",
            "Two ideas help explain the story: changing conditions required new plans, and people with different skills had to work together.",
            "First, our research desk explains the original goal."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Research reporter · the changing goal",
          "voice": "Microsoft David Desktop",
          "visual": "timeline",
          "title": "The ice changed the plan",
          "headline": "THE PLANNED CROSSING DID NOT BEGIN",
          "points": [
            "1914 · Depart for Antarctica",
            "1915 · Ship trapped, then damaged",
            "New priority · Keep the party safe"
          ],
          "credit": "Royal Museums Greenwich chronology; Scott Polar Research Institute",
          "narration": [
            "Shackleton planned to cross Antarctica. Endurance carried his party toward the Weddell Sea, but the ship became trapped in pack ice.",
            "Pack ice is floating sea ice crowded together. The ship drifted with it instead of following the planned route.",
            "When pressure damaged the ship in 1915, the party had to leave it.",
            "That connects a condition to a decision: the damaged ship could no longer provide a safe base for the original mission."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Research reporter · a primary source",
          "voice": "Microsoft David Desktop",
          "visual": "quote",
          "title": "Prepared to move",
          "headline": "READ THE WORDS. EXPLAIN THE EVIDENCE.",
          "points": [
            "Stores, dogs, sledges, and equipment were ready to be moved from the ship at a moment’s notice."
          ],
          "credit": "Ernest Shackleton, South (1919), chapter IV · October 25, 1915 entry",
          "narration": [
            "Here is a short quotation from Shackleton's book South: Stores, dogs, sledges, and equipment were ready to be moved from the ship at a moment's notice.",
            "This detail supports the idea that the group prepared for a change before it happened.",
            "However, this is the leader's account, written after the journey. It helps explain his preparations, but does not tell us what every person thought."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Field reporter · a route toward help",
          "voice": "Microsoft Zira Desktop",
          "visual": "route",
          "title": "A route toward help",
          "headline": "FROM THE ICE TO ELEPHANT ISLAND",
          "points": ["Weddell Sea", "Elephant Island", "South Georgia"],
          "credit": "Classroom schematic · not to scale · South, chapters VIII–IX",
          "narration": [
            "Now look at the route diagram. It begins at the Weddell Sea ice camp, then moves to Elephant Island, then to South Georgia.",
            "In April 1916, the party reached Elephant Island in three small boats.",
            "Six men then took the James Caird toward South Georgia to seek help for those who remained.",
            "The arrows show the order of destinations. They are not exact sailing tracks or a measure of distance."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Field reporter · people and purpose",
          "voice": "Microsoft Zira Desktop",
          "visual": "team",
          "title": "Different skills. A shared purpose.",
          "headline": "PREPARATION CONNECTED PEOPLE’S WORK",
          "points": ["Prepare the boat", "Organize supplies", "Navigate toward help"],
          "credit": "Shackleton, South, chapter IX · The Boat Journey",
          "narration": [
            "The James Caird needed preparation. Carpenter Harry McNish worked on the boat, supplies had to fit into limited space, and Frank Worsley's navigation helped guide the crew.",
            "These were different jobs connected to the same purpose: reaching help.",
            "Preparation could reduce some risks, but it could not make the voyage certain to succeed.",
            "This is why our report explains people's work as well as naming the places they reached."
          ]
        },
        {
          "label": "Research reporter · checking our sources",
          "voice": "Microsoft David Desktop",
          "visual": "sources",
          "title": "What can each source tell us?",
          "headline": "A STRONG REPORT CHECKS MORE THAN ONE SOURCE",
          "points": [
            "Memoir · decisions and experience",
            "Chronology · sequence of events",
            "Photograph · one visible moment"
          ],
          "credit": "South; Royal Museums Greenwich chronology and Frank Hurley photo catalog",
          "narration": [
            "We compared Shackleton's memoir with a museum chronology to check the order of events.",
            "A Frank Hurley photograph, cataloged by Royal Museums Greenwich, is another kind of evidence about the ship and ice.",
            "A photograph records a visible moment. It cannot prove every cause or tell us what someone felt.",
            "Our newsroom background is an illustration, so we do not use it as historical evidence."
          ]
